# Implement joined strokes diacritics and correlated variation

## Scope and existing evidence

Compose accents and joined cursive forms from exact base geometry. Let the
user edit stroke order and direction, reverse a stroke deliberately, and
compare ideal versus varied ink with visible controls for thickness, height,
regularity, spacing and slant.

This is unfinished implementation work. The following original design
records remain accepted theoretical deliverables and are not reopened:

- ATR-P4-005 — archived handwriting design
- ATR-P4-006 — archived handwriting design
- ATR-P4-007 — archived handwriting design

## Ordered-line editing (partial) — 2026-11-03

The browser editor can select an existing segment, reverse its endpoint
direction, and move it before or after the adjacent segment belonging to
the same exact Unicode grapheme, preserving unrelated entries and allowing
explicit TSV roundtrip. Focused tests cover interleaved Unicode glyphs,
first/last boundary disablement, unchanged neighbors, and session reset.
This records **segment order**, not continuous curve grouping, pen lifts,
joined cursive, accent composition or seeded correlated ink variation.
Those acceptance criteria remain open.

## Explicit diacritic addition from authored bases (partial) — 2026-11-03

The glyph editor can append an ordered accent, tilde or diaeresis in the
exact selected Unicode target from existing user-authored base strokes.
Composition keeps the base untouched, copies its stroke sequence to the
target and refuses incompatible targets or clipped accent segments. This
provides an executable, opt-in diacritic composition example; it does not
preserve a live stroke/mark association through later edits. Correlated
seeded variation, joined cursive, multi-stroke pen lifts and continuous
curve primitives are still open.

## Dotless acute i composition (partial) — 2026-11-03

The explicit compose-accent action now handles NFC `í` and NFD `i` plus
combining acute from the already-authored two-stroke dotted lowercase `i`.
It copies the stem unchanged and attaches one bounded acute, omitting only
an independently identifiable short upper dot from the new target. The
original dotted `i` remains unchanged. Unrecognized multi-stroke or
ambiguous dot constructions refuse instead of deleting user ink.

Three focused browser regressions verify the two exact Unicode targets,
source preservation, dotless stroke order, UTF-8 native TSV roundtrip,
refusal of missing/overlapping dots and the opt-in browser composition
workflow. The native Rust glyph CLI read the canonical TSV byte-for-byte,
audited `íí` as **two independent exact Unicode graphemes with two strokes
each and zero missing**, then exported real one-page PDF and transparent
72-DPI RGBA PNG. This is a one-time explicit stroke copy, not
an attached analytic accent or automatic font substitution; cursive and
seeded correlated handwriting remain open.

## Fill-missing Spanish lowercase accents (partial) — 2026-11-03

After the caller explicitly authors the lowercase bases, the localhost
