## Seeded ideal-versus-varied browser ink comparison (partial) — 2026-11-03

The authenticated glyph editor now provides a six-copy SVG comparison of
one exact user-authored Unicode grapheme, including authored accents. The
first row has three ideal repetitions; the second independently varies each
repetition using a caller-specified uint32 seed.

Six bounded spread controls
apply glyph-correlated width, height, thickness and slant, plus per-occurrence
horizontal spacing and endpoint regularity. Shared stroke endpoints derive
jitter from their physical coordinates and seed, so they remain connected
and unaffected by stroke reordering. Invalid amplitudes, seed, zero-length
strokes, clipped pen width/geometry and more than 128 selected strokes
refuse the varied preview instead of changing any authored ink.

Three additional focused regressions cover exact NFC `á` geometry, pen-order
invariance, replay and distinct occurrence seeds, a real browser two-row
accented `ó` preview, spacing-only changes and invalid input refusal.

The TSV downloaded before and after changing the preview seed is byte-for-byte
identical; session end clears the preview. All **75 focused glyph tests**
and the TypeScript check/build pass. An existing real-Firefox/Rust localhost
journey now verifies that authored `ó` produces a 294-line six-copy preview,
seed changes alter only the lower row, and invalid width spread refuses
without deleting the 49 original glyph strokes.

This is not a native page
or PDF/PNG handwriting variation renderer: the seeded samples are
preview-only until individually exported (see below). There is no joined
cursive or persisted correlation across lines or words. The task remains
open for the full finite two-line native output acceptance.

