## Native explicit short stroke connectors (partial) — 2026-11-03

The native glyph CLI supports `--join-strokes GRAPHEME FIRST_INDEX` to insert
an explicitly requested connector between one stroke's pen-end and the next
stroke's pen-start within the **same exact Unicode grapheme**. The one-based
index follows `--inspect-glyph` order; the two segments must share an exact
ink width and RGB, be separated by a nonzero gap of at most 1200 µm, and fit
within the 4096-segment library cap. The original authored endpoints and
neighboring NFC/NFD/other Unicode glyph entries are preserved.

Three Rust tests cover a decomposed-accent glyph interleaved with precomposed
`ñ`, exact TSV inspection and save/reopen, and byte-for-byte PDF/PNG parity
with independently authored connector segments. Invalid indices, missing
neighbors, excessive gaps, already touching endpoints, mismatched color or
width, repeated joins and stroke-budget overflow refuse without modifying
existing source or destination files.

The browser glyph editor also offers **Connect selected stroke to next**
with the same bounded gap, matching pen, capacity and exact-grapheme rules.
It inserts a visible, editable ordered segment into the unsaved draft and
encodes the same canonical TSV accepted by Rust. Browser regressions cover
interleaved Unicode identities, accepted 1200 µm geometry and refusal
without draft mutation.

This is an opt-in **straight bridge**, not a native parametric curve, inferred
continuous pen-lift model or automatic cursive across adjacent letters.
Seeded variation and joined word/line output remain separate unfinished
requirements; the full P1-014 acceptance remains open.

